>
  <p slot="intro">Good Friday in Sorrento is marked by two historic processions, among the most striking in Italy, a tradition traced back to the fourteenth century. In 2027 Easter falls on 28 March, so Good Friday is the 26th.</p>
  <p slot="intro">They are carried by two lay brotherhoods: the Venerable Archconfraternity of Santa Monica, who wear white, and the Venerable Archconfraternity of the Morte, founded in 1586 and heir to an older brotherhood of San Catello documented in 1380, who wear black. Who they are and why they process hooded is covered in our <a href="/en-us/blog/feast-of-the-dead-halloween/" class="text-med-blue hover:underline">piece on the brotherhoods and the cult of the dead</a>.</p>

  <div>
    <h3 class="font-serif text-2xl font-bold text-slate-900 mb-6">The two processions</h3>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
      <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <span class="text-med-gold text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-widest mb-2 block">At three in the morning</span>
        <h4 class="text-xl font-serif font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">The White Procession</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">This is the one of the Addolorata, also called the Visit to the Sepulchres. It leaves the church of the Santissima Annunziata at 3:00am and walks the old town until first light. It stages the Virgin searching for her captured son.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white p-8 rounded-[2rem] border border-slate-100 shadow-sm">
        <span class="text-med-gold text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-widest mb-2 block">At half past eight</span>
        <h4 class="text-xl font-serif font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">The Black Procession</h4>
        <p class="text-slate-600 text-xs sm:text-sm leading-relaxed">This is the one of the Dead Christ, carried by the Archconfraternity of the Morte. It sets off at 8:30pm with black hoods, a funeral march and the body borne through the centre. It is the larger of the two.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</EventPageLayout>
